ork so the goal is to  reduce the amount of work that braiding  programs have to do each season and  that's where near-infrared spectroscopy  comes in and this has been used before  in cassava but I'll explain a little bit  about what it is so this is looking at  the reflectance in the near infrared  region of the electromagnetic spectrum  this is 780 to 2500 nanometers so just  beyond the visible it's measured by  using an active light source that emits  wavelengths in this range on a  biological sample and then the sample  itself changes the reflectance pattern  that's sensed by a sensor and so this is  what cassava looks like when you scan it  with a near infrared spectrometer so we  see reflectance on the y axis and  wavelengths on the X and this tells us  about the chemical and physical  properties of the sample that was  scanned but the signal that comes back  is complex and so it requires  multivariate statistical techniques to  decipher what's actually going on but  once you have models calibrated for the  traits of interest you can measure these  traits quickly and non-destructively so  like I said it's been used before in  cassava to look at dry matter content in  roots this has been done first of all  with the 6500 this is a benchtop model  that's been shown to be highly  predictive of dry matter content so  above 0.94 in the literature and then we  have a spectral range here that includes  some some of the visible range as well  from 420 580 meters the next generation  was from EK sorry Google Yahoo from John  Luke's lab and he looked at this mobile  spectrometer the quality spectra it has  an even larger spectral range but the  predictive ability dropped a little bit  in his tests to 0.8 which is still  pretty good and it passed the standards  for breeders but the problem with these  two units is they're really expensive  and for a small breeding program this  might be way out of the budget you could  extra workers you could put extra field  plots do more trials for this amount of  money so for these reasons we've been  looking into the CEO which is a small  hand handheld spectrometer that's only  three hundred dollars per unit but it  does have a smaller spectral range and  this this means that we need to evaluate  the performance of the spectrometer  before we can fully recommend it to  breeding programs in order to do that we  collect calibration data so scans from  the spectrometer paired with these oven  measurements that I was talking about  before we use those as input for  prediction model development and once we  have models that pass our standards we  can use them routinely in breeding  programs but to get to that point we  need to optimize the protocols that  we're using both in actual sampling of  the roots and in model development and  then we do need to keep going back and  making sure that our models are still  appropriate as our populations change we  have three generations so the  calibration data collection looks like  selecting several routes for plot and  then slicing them so this you have three  different slice points and that is that  is so that we can cover the variation  within the route these route slices are  each scanned with the spectrometer and  then we take a mean plot dry matter  content measurement using the oven  method that I talked about earlier and  these scans are paired with these  measurements and those go into our  prediction models so these are the  populations that I've been working with  so far the dry matter content varies  pretty widely both within and among both  trials and breeding programs I want to  point out a couple things here first is  these two trials a timber Appa and  Brazil are seedling evaluation trials  and if you talk to any cassava breeder  you'll know that seedling evaluation  trials from true seed are not always  indicative of what the plant will  actually do when you take a clonally a  clonal probably from it so we see a wide  range of dry matter content in those two  these two trials from my ITA are  actually the same genotypes grown in two  different years so we do see a lot of  variation due to environment here and  then this trial at nakri is has a low  tail here for dry matter content and  that is because of CBS d incidents in  Uganda so that's those disease routes  from that first slide that I showed and  that really affects the dry matter  content of okay so once we have these  oven dry matter content measurements  paired with our scans we can use those  to create prediction models so I divide  them into training and test sets and  then use five fold cross validation and  partially to tune partially squares  model parameters with the training set  and then use the optimized model to  predict our training star test set I  output model performance statistics like  R squared root mean squared error and  then I repeat this with different  subsets of training and test sets so  when we look at performance within  breeding programs so that's the diagonal  this is not a correlation matrix it is a  training set on the y-axis test set on  the X this is whole breeding program  performance so we can see up in the top  left is using AIT a data to predict AIT  a data and then so on so this is in 2018  we see that model performance is pretty  high we got a 0.85 r squared with a 2.6  our MSE for i-i-i-t a and then going  down there but the real test would be to  see how the models perform when your  training on one breeding program and  testing another so can we train models  at iit a and test them in rwanda say  answer is mixed back we have some models  doing really well like using the  improper data to predict the plots at  nakri but then when you use iit a we're  getting poor performance predicting  either of the other breeding  programs this is a little concerning but  again we could just train models for  each individual breeding program ideally  though we would use everything we have  and training like a major model and then  use that to test everything and so when  we do that we actually see the  performance go back up which is pretty  great so this is really comparable to  this diagonal here we only lose a very  small amount of our predictive ability  but in order to make these big models  using everything we have we have to be  able to put everything together in one  place  so this is where cassava base comes in  I've been working with Lukas Mueller's  group to create the infrastructure to  store an access spectral data within  cassava base this will allow for us to  take training and test sets from any any  trial within the database and then use  them to build and evaluate models so the  functions that I've been writing and  using from my own work we're putting  into cassava base for use by any breeder  and then they can build evaluate their  own models and use them for prediction  and then the predicted value is can be  used for selection in the selection  indices I've been talking about so  that's our plan moving forward but I'd  really like to thank all of the next-gen  cassava breeding programs Gore lab the  pheno apps team my committee and then  the funding from the Vino apps project  so that I'll take questions yeah Evan so  by combining - you mean two programs or  yeah so um here what I did is trained on  two programs and like 70% of the third  and then tests on that 30% hold out so I  haven't tried training a model on say  IIT and a Korean predicting Embrapa but  that's definitely something that I could  do yeah so for here I'm just training it  on the spectra but I do have a test  plots from all of the environments  included in the training set so I also  have done cross validation schemes that  hold out an entire environment or  specific genotypes and those as you'd  expect perform a little more poorly than  these do yeah it is pretty exciting  yeah so the question was could I subset  the data from the spectrometers with a  larger range to my smaller range and  